WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.880
This... is the Schrodinger equation.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.460
I know this looks ugly but don’t run away!

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.910
I’m going to talk about what the Schrodinger
equation actually means, and hopefully you’ll

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:14.320
see it doesn’t deserve its bad reputation.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:15.700
Physics tries to predict the future.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.710
What I mean is, if you have an object in some
state that you know, and then you do something

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.149
to it- you let it go above the ground, or
you give it a big push, or you put it next

00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:30.059
to something electric, the job of physics
is to tell you, what happens to the object

00:00:30.059 --> 00:00:31.640
next?

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.650
Newtonian Mechanics did a good job of that.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.430
If you know the initial position and velocity
of an object, it will tell you exactly where

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:42.470
the particle is any time in the future.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:47.740
In other words, you give it the initial state
of the object, and based surroundings it give

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.710
you the state at time t.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:55.320
What we want for quantum mechanics is something
that will do the same- tell us what the future

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.140
state of the system is.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.579
That’s what the Schrodinger equation is
for.

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:06.649
In fact there’s a much nicer way to write
the equation that puts it into this form.

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:10.799
Remember the state of a quantum particle is
given by the wavefunction.

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:15.189
So if this is the wavefunction at the start,
the Schrodinger equation tells you that this

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:20.409
thing acts on it to produce the wavefunction
t seconds into the future.

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:25.750
Then the question is, what is this transformation,
and why is it that way?

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.750
If you’re interested in the history of quantum
mechanics then you may have heard Schrodinger

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.880
essentially just came down from the mountains
with his fully formed equation, with no derivation.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.130
This is kind of true- but that doesn’t mean
there is no way to motivate the Schrodinger

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.280
equation these days.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.289
The main thing it comes down to is conservation
of energy.

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:51.799
It’s surprisingly straight forward, but
I decided to put that derivation of the equation

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:53.170
to the next video.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:58.829
See, the derivation of the equation, and how
to precisely use the it are good to know but

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:01.779
kind of optional in terms of what we’ll
do later.

00:02:01.779 --> 00:02:06.939
However there is one fact about the equation
that is so vital we will use it again and

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:08.920
again in future videos.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.940
That one fact is so important, it’s all
we’ll need to understand quantum computers,

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:19.060
and prove the no cloning theorem and quantum
teleportation, and discuss the many worlds

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.780
interpretation of quantum mechanics, plus
basically everything else we’re going to

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:23.780
do.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.390
So here it is: time evolution is linear.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.310
Let me explain that.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.810
Suppose that I have an object and there are
two different states I can start the object

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:34.810
in.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.540
For example, I could just drop this object
so it has no velocity to begin, or I could

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.790
throw it, so it has a big starting velocity.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:46.370
We know what would happen to the object in
each case, right?

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.650
We apply time evolution to each, and this
is how they evolve.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:56.320
But now suppose my object didn’t start in
one of these 2 states -but actually a superposition

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.460
of both.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:03.690
In this case, it both had no velocity and
a big velocity to start.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.780
What happens to it over time?

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.650
What do you expect happens?

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.320
Think about it.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:18.250
Hopefully, what you expect is that it will
be a superposition of the evolution of each

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.990
state, so at each point in time, the state
looks like this.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:29.430
The mathematical way to put this is, the time
evolution of a superposition is the linear

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:33.130
combination of the time evolution of the each
of the states.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.960
This is what I mean.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.230
If I have a state in any superposition like
this, to get the overall future state, I just

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:45.650
look at the future state of the individual
parts and add them back up.

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:50.220
It even holds if you write you that same state
in a different basis.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:56.130
For example if I changed to the momentum basis
here, the same rule applies, and so these

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:02.120
two states are equal to each other.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.460
So the general statement of linearity is this:

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.400
This does seem like a reasonable thing to
request from quantum mechanics, but linearity

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.940
has some subtle philosophical statements about
superposition hidden in it.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.290
Look back at this example.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:24.690
It says that, these separate parts of the
superposition don’t act like they know the

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:26.840
other part is there.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.180
This part of the object here doesn’t look
over its shoulder and see that there’s another

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.750
part of the superposition and decide to do
something else.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.630
Regardless of whether there is another state
in the superposition or not, this part of

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.820
the superposition does the same thing.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.770
In other words, anything in this part of the
superposition evolves completely unaware of

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.620
the other parts.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:54.090
Well, that is until measurement happens.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.920
When you measure, the branches of the superposition
have the chance to interact which each other

00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.920
again.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.800
That’s because measurement doesn’t follow
the Schrodinger equation.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.890
This fact, that ‘measurement’ doesn’t
obey the same rule that everything else in

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:13.750
the universe is subject to, is so bizarre,
if not down right contradictory that it has

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.360
it’s own name: the measurement problem,
and it’s the biggest foundational issue

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.010
in quantum mechanics.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:25.930
Meanwhile, everything else evolves perfectly
linearly in time, unaware of the other parts

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:29.270
of the superposition.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:32.960
If you want to know where linearity comes
from and what might have inspired the Schrodinger

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:36.610
equation in the first place, click this link
to the next video.

